CONSPECTUS: Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) and porous coordination polymers (PCPs) have been well-recognized as emerging porous materials that afford highly tailorable and well-defined nanoporous structures with threedimensional lattices. Because of their microporous nature, MOFs can accommodate small molecules in their lattice structure, thus discriminating them on the basis of their size and physical properties and enabling their separation even in the gas phase. Such characteristics of MOFs have attracted significant attention in recent years for diverse applications and have ignited a worldwide race toward their development in both academic and industrial fields. Most recently, new challenges in porous materials science demand processable liquid, melt, and amorphous forms of MOFs. This trend will provide a new fundamental class of microporous materials for further widespread applications in many fields. In particular, the application of flexible membranes for gas separation is expected as an efficient solution to tackle current energy-intensive issues. To date, amorphous MOFs have been prepared in a top-down approach by the introduction of disorder into the parent frameworks. However, this new paradigm is still in its infancy with respect to the rational design principles that need to be developed for any approach that may include bottom-up synthesis of porous soft materials.
Herein we describe recent progress in bottom-up “modular” approaches for the synthesis of porous, processable MOF-based materials, wherein metal−organic cages (MOCs), alternatively called metal−organic polyhedra (MOPs), are used as “modular cavities” to build porous soft materials. The outer periphery of a MOP is decorated with polymeric and dendritic side chains to obtain a polymer-grafted MOP, imparting both solution and thermal processability to the MOP cages, which have an inherent nanocavity along with high tailorability analogous to MOFs. Well-ordered MOP assemblies can be designed to obtain phases ranging from crystals to liquid crystals, allowing the fabrication of flexible free-standing sheets with preservation of the longrange ordering of MOPs.
Furthermore, future prospects of the modular design for porous soft materials are provided with the anticipation that the bottom-up design will combine porous materials and soft matter sciences, leading to the discovery and development of many unexplored new materials and devices such as MOF-based self-healing membranes possessing well-defined nanochannels. The macroscopic alignment of channels can be controlled by external factors, including electric and magnetic fields, external forces, and modified surfaces (templating and patterning), which are conventionally used for engineering of soft materials.
## 1. INTRODUCTION
Porous materials, such as porous carbons, zeolites, metal− organic frameworks (MOFs), covalent organic frameworks (COFs), and porous polymers, are an important class of materials that are useful for molecular separation as well as storage and catalysis.1 The microporosity of such porous materials imparts them with many useful functionalities arising from their ability to adsorb and capture molecules and sieve/ sort them according to their sizes and shapes. MOFs, alternatively termed porous coordination polymers (PCPs) in view of their porosity, have emerged as potential nanoporous materials that offer high degrees of freedom in designing their framework and pore structures.2−4 Those features of MOFs have engendered the rapid growth of chemistry in the coordination nanospace, which not only allows the capture5 and discrimination of guest molecules6 but also enables their synthesis and conversion into other value-added substances via nanomodulated reactions in the confined space.7 Considering the practical industrial applications of MOFs, one has to consider the optimization of their processability to tailor them into different forms, such as pellets, foams, and membranes, to meet the demands of their diverse applications. However, most MOFs are obtained in the crystalline state and are mechanically rigid and hard, often precluding rational access to such processable materials, which has led to a new wave of research activity focusing on the discovery of liquid and amorphous states of MOFs.
Amorphous MOFs have recently attracted scientific interest. When MOF crystals collapse upon application of pressure, variation of the temperature, or ball-milling, defects are formed in the crystals at the molecular and mesoscopic scales, eventually rendering them noncrystalline or turning them into a glass form. Recently, such “defective by design” MOFs have been proposed to realize glass/amorphous MOFs and are recognized as a new category of processable MOFs (Figure 1).8−10 This top-down approach of disrupting the framework into a primary coordination network structure has led to the discovery of melting MOFs. The amorphous MOFs can potentially be applied as processable porous materials for trapping of guest molecules11 and as proton-conductive media12 as well as for storage and separation, as has been proposed earlier.8 Such defects and accompanying disorders in MOFs can be further engineered not only to have segregated nanodomain structures or homogeneous alloys of different MOFs in a single material, which will be useful for the design of catalytic sites, but also to control transport properties in applications in which percolation critically depends on the positioning of the correlated defects.13
To overcome the aforementioned issues related to the processing of MOFs, we have proposed a different approach, viz., “modular design”, that focuses on bottom-up synthesis using metal−organic cages (MOCs), alternatively termed metal−organic polyhedra (MOPs), as a modular cavity component of porous soft materials (Figure 1). The bottomup approach would allow us to combine MOFs with soft matter sciences to control the dimensions and alignment of the nanocavities formed after self-organization, thus providing a wider prospect for the future development and application of MOFs that have rarely been considered on the track of MOF sciences to date. The modular cage compounds can be modified with diverse molecular functionalities and polymers, thus enabling us to control their physical properties and processabilities, molecular packing structures, and macroscopic alignment of the cage modules. This will lead to further developments in not only self-healing porous materials but also molecular separation and transport membranes through the versatile dimensional control over self-organization.
In the present Account, we highlight the recent progress in the design and synthesis of the cavity modules and provide future perspectives on this modular approach based on the selforganization of metal−organic cages.
## 2. BOTTOM-UP APPROACHES FOR POROUS SOFTMATERIALS
Robust cagelike compounds with accessible nanocavities can be suitable constituent modules for the bottom-up approach. Recently, liquid porous materials have been demonstrated using organic cage compounds.14,15 The porous liquid system consists of multiple components wherein the organic cages are dissolved in selected solvents; the molecular sizes of these solvents are larger than that of the cage window and they are incapable of occupying the cavity of the cage. The cage in such a mixture functions like a modular cavity even in the liquid phase. This example demonstrates the promise of the bottomup approach and has motivated us to construct a new class of porous soft materials based on MOPs serving as modular cavities, analogous to the nanospaces of MOFs.
MOPs are discrete metal−organic cage compounds formed via the self-assembly of multiple metal ions and organic ligands (Figure 2a).16−20 A variety of metal ions and ligands with diverse structures afford many kinds of cages with a welldefined cavity within their core, and the size of the cavity ranges from 1 to $2\ \mathrm{nm}$ . To date, MOPs have been widely studied as motifs of nanochannels,21,22 nanocontainers,23 and porous materials;24 they are used in their crystalline state and incorporated as a building component in MOFs.
The underlying technical challenges in the exploitation of MOPs are their poor solubility, poor processability, and high crystallinity due to strong intermolecular interactions. These issues can be overcome by the direct coupling of MOPs with polymers to improve their solubility and to endow them with the thermoplastic property in order to render them processable (Figure 2b). Several groups have attempted to couple polymeric compounds with MOPs to obtain branched (star) and cross-linked architectures wherein the MOPs act as the junctions. The highly symmetrical shape of MOPs and the geometrical positioning of the metal ions and ligands make them suitable building blocks as the junction points of such branching and cross-linking molecular structures. Several MOP motifs have been used to fabricate MOP-core (branched) macromolecules as discrete porous modules that will be mainly highlighted in this Account (Figure 3a,b).25,26 The network polymers cross-linked via MOPs form either gels or solid materials in the dried state with a persistent shape ,27−31 which are advantageous as mechanically stable membranes and porous monolithic materials.32,33 Such MOP-linked structures have been obtained by “polymer first” and “MOP first” approaches. The former involves the use of telechelic ligands for the successive formation of MOPs to produce a network structure (Figure 3c,d), whereas the latter involves the synthesis of MOPs followed by inter-MOP cross-linking with bridging additives (Figure 3e). Compared to MOF-based gels and porous monoliths developed to date,34 in which crystalline MOF nanoparticles with diameters of a few tens of nanometers are aggregated, the MOP-based ones are composed of much smaller and discrete cavity units that are randomly associated, resulting in a porous amorphous material.
The MOP-core branched macromolecules can also be synthesized via two approaches: (1) the “MOP first” approach involving graft polymerization from the MOP surface and (2)
the “polymer first” approach involving postpolymerization coupling or assembly. Both pathways have been successfully used to produce MOPs coupled with polymeric arms as side chains, hereafter termed polymer-grafted MOPs (or alternatively, coordination star polymers (CSPs) in view of the macromolecular topology; vide infra).25
## 3. DESIGN AND SYNTHESIS OF POLYMER-GRAFTED MOPS
## 3.1. Graft Polymerization: “MOP First”
MOPs are formed via coordination-driven self-assembly, which allows the formation of only a single product from multiple components. A representative structure of a MOP is the rhombicuboctahedron consisting of 24 isophthalates interconnected with 12 copper paddlewheel nodes.16−19 To modify the surface of the MOP with polymeric compounds, a living radical polymerization technique using the MOP as the reaction core has been demonstrated.25 Reversible addition− fragmentation transfer (RAFT) polymerization was employed to prevent the use of a metal catalyst that could disturb the coordination framework of the MOP. An isophthalic acid ligand substituted with dithiobenzoate trithioester $(Z={\cal S}{\cal C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{9},$ hereafter termed as 1), a typical chain transfer (CT) moiety for RAFT, was designed to be used in the synthesis of $\mathbf{MOPs}$ through the coordination reaction with copper(II) (Figure 4). The synthesized MOPs, denoted as $\mathbf{MOP}(\mathbf{1}).$ , allowed MOPmediated RAFT polymerization of tert-butyl acrylate (tBA) to successfully afford the growth of polymer chains from the MOP core in a living fashion, yielding the polymer-grafted MOPs, MOP(1)-PtBA. The polymer-grafted MOPs thus obtained have 24 grafted arm chains, which originate from the MOP structure with 24 isophthalate units.
This “MOP first” synthetic route allows not only homopolymers but also block copolymers to be grafted on the MOP core by stepwise polymerization reactions, leading to a core−shell-type polymer-grafting architecture.
## 3.2. Self-Assembly of Macroligands: “Polymer First”
In the “polymer first” approach, the MOPs are constructed after the polymerization of the polymer ligand (hereafter termed the macroligand) to obtain polymer-grafted MOPs using rather simple and easy protocols. The self-assembly of the MOP core leads to a single product with a highly symmetrical shape that strictly defines the polymer-grafted architecture.25 (Figure 5).
A macroligand possessing an isophthalic acid at one end of its side is synthesized by a conventional living radical polymerization technique or common coupling reaction (Figure 5a). Upon mixing with copper(II) acetate in a common solvent such as $N,N$ -dimethylformamide (DMF), $N$ -methylpyrrolidone (NMP), or tetrahydrofuran (THF), the macroligands, e.g., PtBA, instantly converge into the polymergrafted MOP, which is denoted as $\mathbf{MOP(PtBA)}$ , via the selfassembly of isophthalates and copper(II) ions (Figure 5b).
The resultant polymer-grafted MOPs have a structure identical to that synthesized by the MOP-first approach. It is more difficult to assemble macroligands with higher molecular weights into polymer-grafted MOPs using the polymer-first approach because of the steric effect. In this regard, the MOPfirst approach is more advantageous for the synthesis of highermolecular-weight MOPs, whereas the polymer-first approach is more feasible and applicable for the synthesis of versatile MOPs. Hence, those two approaches can be used complementarily.
By means of the polymer-first approach, a variety of MOPs decorated with different polymer chains can be synthesized, which is impossible to achieve via the MOP-first approach. For example, a mixture of different macroligands, PtBA and poly( $n$ -butyl acrylate) $\mathbf{\left(PnBA\right)}$ , can be used to produce a single polymer-grafted MOP with mixed arm chains (Figure 5c). From the viewpoint of synthesizing star polymers and branch polymers, which always involve multistep and difficult procedures, the synthesis of heteroarm MOPs via the polymer-first approach is quite advantageous as it does not require complex techniques and allows the components to be easily tailored, although the incorporation number of each component is defined as the statistical average of the entire material. In view of this aspect, polymer-grafted MOPs may be differently termed as coordination star polymers (CSPs), constituting a new class of branched macromolecules.25
In addition to vinyl polymers, the polymer-first approach is applicable to a variety of other polymers. For instance, the coupling reaction between isophthalic acid and poly(ethylene glycol) (PEG) also generates a useful macroligand. PEGgrafted MOPs can be constructed with the PEG macroligand, enabling many possible versatile applications based on them. Interestingly, it is found that the bulky and highly branched morphology (topology) of the polymer-grafted MOP suppresses the entanglement and prevents the crystallization of grafted side chains, yielding new soft materials with interesting physical and mechanical properties.
## 3.3. Processability and the Structure of the Assembly in the Bulk State
The expected core−shell-like microscopic structure of the polymer-grafted MOPs has been directly observed by atomic force microscopy (AFM). Each polymer-grafted MOP was found to have a single MOP core surrounded by a polymeric corona (Figure 6a).25
The bulk properties of polymer-grafted MOPs can be tuned by changing the grafted polymers. A MOP with poly( $n$ -butyl acrylate) side chains, $\mathbf{MOP(PnBA)}$ , has a low glass transition temperature $(T_{\mathrm{g}})$ of $-43.7^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and behaves like a fluid at room temperature, although in the liquid form $\mathbf{MOP(PnBA)}$ has a self-organized structure that was confirmed by small-angle Xray scattering (SAXS) analysis. The SAXS profile of the MOPs showed a peak, suggesting the dense packing structure of the MOPs with regular intercore distances (d spacing) (Figure 6b). The intercore distances of the self-organized MOP cores could be tuned by tailoring the length of the polymer arm chains. For example, the $d$ spacings of MOP(PnBA14) (degree of polymerization (DP) of $\mathbf{PnBA}=14$ ) and MOP(PnBA56) (DP of $\mathbf{PnBA}=\mathbf{\nabla}56\mathbf{\Omega}.$ ) were found to be 4.2 and $6.4\ \mathrm{nm}$ respectively. Whereas $\mathbf{MOP(PnBA)}$ is fluidic, MOPs decorated with poly(methyl acrylate) form a soft plastic material at room temperature, which can be solution-cast to afford a freestanding sheet (Figure 1).
## 4. STRUCTURAL VARIETY OF POLYMER-GRAFTED MOPS
## 4.1. Self-Sorting Synthesis of Anisotropic Cage−Core Macromolecules
Modular pore design using metal−organic cages has many prospects not only in terms of fabricating liquid and soft porous materials but also in the ubiquitous design of pore (channel) ordering and anisotropic alignment on a macroscopic scale using self-organization principles. As a fundamental tool for these future developments, anisotropic functionalization of MOPs is essential to induce the directed selforganization phenomenon. To fulfill this requirement, selfsorting-based synthesis of MOPs is an effective strategy. Although many MOPs comprising multiple types of ligands have been reported, the self-sorting phenomenon upon MOP formation has not been explored to a significant extent to date. Macroligands with isophthalic termini form the typical rhombicuboctahedral MOPs with an isotropic spherical shape. This self-assembly system could be modulated by adding a dicarboxylic coligand, azobenzene- $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid (Azo), to the reaction mixture, thereby leading to the formation of another MOP core structure with an anisotropic shape (Figure 7).35 This results in a polymer-grafted MOP with segregated arm chains attached at the polar axis of the MOP core.
The AzoMOP core has three isophthalate units at each polar position, totaling six, and six Azo groups at the equatorial positions. Therefore, the macroligands are attached only at the polar positions, and a total of six chains are grafted onto the AzoMOP core. The macroligands do not form the isotropic cuboctahedral MOP in the presence of the Azo coligand because of the balance between the steric effect of the arm chains and the difference in the stability between the cuboctahedral and anisotropic MOP cores. This eventually makes the anisotropic MOP core energetically favorable in this self-sorting system. This self-sorting approach could be a powerful tool in the synthesis of anisotropic architectures of polymer-grafted MOPs, as many heteroleptic MOP cores have been already reported (Figure 8).
## 4.2. Dendronized MOPs
Polymer arm chains may occupy and penetrate through the cage cavity, eventually hindering the pore functions of the MOP core. One approach to address this issue is to use a dendrimer that does not fit into the cage window, which could preserve the function of the cavity of the MOP in the bulk phase. Dendronized MOPs were synthesized using an anisotropic MOP core (Figure 9).36 Carbazole-based dendritic ligands G2 and G3 possessing an isophthalic acid moiety at the focal point were used to produce dendronized MOPs via selfsorting between the dendritic ligand and a dicarboxylic coligand, 3,3-(ethyne-1,2-diyl)dibenzoate (Tol), in the presence of copper(II) ions. Tol has a structure similar to that of the Azo ligand and is known to yield an anisotropic MOP architecture, TolMOP (Figure 9), when reacted with isophthalate and copper(II) ions.19 The self-assembly of G2 and Tol resulted in TolMOP(G2) possessing three G2 dendrons at each polar position of the MOP core. The structure of TolMOP(G2) was unambiguously determined by single-crystal X-ray diffraction analyses.36
The much larger ligand G3 could also provide the same MOP architecture, demonstrating the potential of this selfsorting approach for the facile synthesis of nanosized macromolecules. Importantly, the dendritic ligands G2 and G3 do not produce isotropic cuboctahedral MOPs. The cuboctahedral MOP requires the congregation of a total of 24 isophthalates into a single MOP structure, which results in increased steric repulsion of the dendritic ligands. This steric repulsion eventually destabilizes the isotropic cuboctahedral MOP structure, thereby suppressing the formation of the isotropic byproduct as a consequence.
## 5. SELF-ORGANIZATION OF MOPS INTO HIERARCHICAL STRUCTURES
Further hierarchical arrangement of the MOP module into a bulk material is the next challenge for macroscopic ordering and alignment of the resultant porous soft materials. Designing liquid-crystalline materials can be a promising strategy as it involves rational control over the packing and arrangement of molecules at a macroscopic scale.
Porous liquid-crystalline materials and liquid-crystalline polymer networks, which possess molecular nanochannels and highly ordered structures in a single material, have been studied by many groups. Supramolecular liquid crystals (LCs) and some macrocyclic compounds have been designed to construct nanochannels in the LC phase.37−39 These examples successfully demonstrate the potential of porous LCs to be applied as adsorbents and membranes for molecular separation.
To apply the LC design to a MOP system, the cuboctahedral MOPs were densely decorated with paraffinic chains on the surface, reducing the intermolecular interactions between the MOPs and controlling the packing structures to produce a lyotropic LC phase with the self-organized structures of MOPs, where the MOPs act as both the mesogenic component and cavity module (Figure 10).40
MOPs consisting of isophthalate ligands with aliphatic chains, ligands a and b, were synthesized and found to exhibit long-range ordering with a lyotropic mesophase in the presence of $N,N.$ -dimethylacetamide (DMA) (Figure 10a). The powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the MOPs indicated a body-centered cubic (bcc) packing structure (Figure 10b). The bcc lattice of MOP(a) has an edge length of $4.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ and cell diagonal distance of $7.8~\mathrm{nm}$ , from which the proximal distance between the MOP cores is estimated to be $3.9\ \mathrm{nm}$ . MOP(b) consisting of much longer aliphatic chains showed a similar long-range bcc packing structure with a much larger lattice constant of $4.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ .
The paraffinic MOPs yielded a freestanding sheet without the loss of their three-dimensional long-range ordering structure (Figure 10c).40 A smooth macroscopic sheet comprising MOPs, with the bcc packing structure preserved, was obtained by a solution layering method followed by slow evaporation. The LC feature of the MOPs would allow their macroscopic ordering under external forces such as the application of an electric or magnetic field, stretching, and molecular templating. Thus, this method is a powerful tool for designing molecular separation media with well-defined gas diffusion channels.
It should be noted that there is still a technical difficulty in measuring the accessible void space of such soft porous materials because common gas-adsorption-based porosity measurements are not always valid for polymeric soft materials that take up gases by solvation, which might lead to misreading of the surface area. Positron annihilation lifetime spectroscopy (PALS) could be an effective method to study the cavity structure. In addition, control of disorder would be critical in MOP-based porous soft materials and is already considered essential to define functionalities and performance of crystalline/amorphous MOFs.13 The development of measurement methods as well as theoretical models and computational analyses to assess the intrinsic porosity and extent of defect/ disordered structure will be beneficial for characterizations in the future.
## 6. PERSPECTIVES FOR THE MODULAR DESIGN OF POROUS SOFT MATERIALS
One of the promising applications of porous soft materials is in molecular separation, and they can also serve as ion conduction membranes. Membrane-based gas separation is recognized as one of the promising solutions for tackling the current energy-intensive issues of molecular separation processes. The use of mixed-matrix membranes (MMMs) consisting of MOFs and polymers is a kind of approach to achieve a satisfactory level of processability of MOFs to be used as gas permeation channels.41 However, crystalline MOFs still hinder the fabrication of mechanically stable, defect-free MMMs, which compels one to struggle with homogeneous dispersions of MOF particles in a polymeric matrix.42 Incompatibility between two different substances of this type leads to physical gaps between the MOF−polymer interface, eventually resulting in low separation performance. Hence, MMM applications of MOFs demand a paradigm-shifting approach in terms of defect-free synthesis and channel design for higher separation performance.
The modular synthesis described herein inspires the discovery of unique materials and provides sophisticated membrane designs that are otherwise not accessible via conventional porous material synthesis. Some future perspectives and applications that we propose are illustrated in Figure 11. The fabrication of porous materials via modular synthesis can be categorized in terms of the dimensions of the organized structures.
## 6.1. Homogeneous Organization (Zero-Dimensional)
Materials with zero-dimensional (0D) pores could provide fundamental processable porous soft materials that can serve as defect-free adsorbent/separation media (Figure 11a).43 Selfhealing porous materials can be expected, as have already been well-demonstrated in some polymeric materials by exploiting reversible bonding.44 This can be applied to the modular design we have proposed, which could especially contribute to addressing typical crack-induced malfunctions of gas separation membranes. Furthermore, the processable characteristics would enable injecting, (3D) printing, patterning, and reforming/recycling of porous materials.
## 6.2. Anisotropic Organization (One-Dimensional)
1D self-assembly of porous modules yields nanochannels. 1D design affords molecular/charge transport, and there are already several reports on the use of MOPs as artificial ion channels in lipid bilayer membranes.21,22 Sequential lamination of such membranes with different MOPs (e.g., different cavity sizes, intercavity hydrophobicities, and polarities) are expected to provide a physical gradient that enables unidirectional mass transport and diffusion (Figure 11b). This could likewise be applied for catalytic functions in unique membrane reactors.
## 6.3. Asymmetric and Directional Organization (Two-Dimensional)
Directed self-assembly (DSA) technology is a tool to control the self-organized 2D structure and alignment, leading to the rational design of separation/conduction membranes and also microelectromechanical systems (MEMS) and sensors (Figure 11c). DSA has emerged as a promising technique for the fabrication of various nanostructures and patterns with a length scale of a few to hundreds of nanometers.45 The obtained nanostructures, e.g., lamellar, cylindrical, and spherical phases, can be ordered and aligned under shear, an electric field, or a magnetic field and also by employing temperature gradient (zone annealing) and gradient coating (zone coating) methods.45,46 This ordering can yield nanostructured membranes that have cage-segregated channels serving as gas permeation and ion conduction paths.
## 6.4. Hierarchical Organization (Three-Dimensional)
A wide range of materials that can serve not only as gas separation/capture media but also as materials with unique optical and mechanical properties are expected from the hierarchical 3D self-organization of porous modules (Figure 11d). A system with a long-range-ordered structure consisting of channels and cavities can be designed by the LC-based approach. Cross-linking of cage modules has been used to obtain 3D-shaped materials that can serve as drug delivery vehicles,29 gas/molecular adsorbents,32,33 and platforms for network polymers with unique topologies and rheological/ mechanical properties.27,28
There are many possible future prospects for the porous soft materials. The bottom-up approach by cage self-organization offers new dimensions of flexibility and processability for porous materials that will enable the construction of fourthgeneration porous materials.3 We anticipate that the combination of porous materials and soft matter sciences will further guide us in the discovery and development of many unexplored new materials and devices. We believe that the present Account will further inspire and ignite the community to step forward into the emerging research area of porous soft materials.
